<title>BRAZIL: Brazil coffee group hires lawyer to study EU tax.</title>
<headline>Brazil coffee group hires lawyer to study EU tax.</headline>
<dateline>SAO PAULO 1996-12-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil's Soluble Coffee Association (ABICS) is hiring a lawyer to study whether the EU is using an import duty on Brazilian soluble coffee to push Brazil out of the European market, the ABICS president said.</p>
<p>&quot;We are hiring a lawyer who will begin the study and who will produce a report on whether we have a case,&quot; Sergio Coimbra said at a lunch with journalists.</p>
<p>The tax on soluble coffee is expected to rise to 10.1 percent in 1997 from nine percent. Such coffee producers as Bolivia, Peru, Ecuador and Colombia are exempt from the tax, Coimbra said.</p>
<p>Coimbra said that the study, which should be ready in three to four months, already has the support of Brazilian Foreign Affairs Minister Luiz Felipe Lampreia.</p>
<p>-- Romina Nicaretta, Sao Paulo newsroom, 5511 248 5415</p>
